<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">12 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 12 March, from 1088 to 1989.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1088 CE <span class="tih-ago">938 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Odo of Châtillon elected Pope Urban II</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Elected at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terracina">Terracina</a> while a rival pope held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Urban II</a> continued Gregory VII's reforms. In 1095 he preached the First Crusade at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-clermont">Council of Clermont</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1664 CE <span class="tih-ago">362 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Charles II grants New Netherland to the Duke of York</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The King granted his brother James lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> and Delaware rivers, then held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. English ships seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> that August and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1912 CE <span class="tih-ago">114 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Juliette Gordon Low founds the Girl Scouts in Savannah</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Inspired by the Guide movement she had seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Low gathered 18 girls for the first meeting of the American Girl Guides. Renamed the Girl Scouts in 1913, it has had tens of millions of members.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1938 CE <span class="tih-ago">88 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">German troops march into Austria</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> crossed the border unopposed and was greeted by cheering crowds. The next day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was declared part of the German Reich, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> had forbidden.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1947 CE <span class="tih-ago">79 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Truman announces the Truman Doctrine</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Asking Congress for aid to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, Harry Truman pledged to support 'free peoples who are resisting attempted subjugation'. The doctrine framed US <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> policy of containment.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1989 CE <span class="tih-ago">37 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Tim Berners-Lee proposes the World Wide Web</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cern">CERN</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, Berners-Lee circulated 'Information Management: A Proposal' for a hypertext system linking documents across computers. His supervisor called it 'vague but exciting'.</p></div></li></ol>
